//
// @EXPECT_ERROR: discharge the null where it is produced
//
// Re-storing a nullable result into the non-null variable it came from must name a
// cure that WORKS.
//
// The refusal is right: a variable divisor can be zero, so `x / g` is `float?` (C80),
// and storing that into a `float` slot is a real narrowing. What was wrong is that the
// general re-type message offered `as` and a new variable name, and on THIS shape both
// fail — `as float` is refused by the cast checker for the very reason the store was,
// `as float?` lands back on this same error (a closed loop between two diagnostics),
// and a fresh name still has to store the value somewhere. The cures that work — `?`
// and `?? <default>` — were named only by the cast checker, never here (loft#859).
//
// The trigger is a nullable-producing operator (`/`, `%`) with a non-literal right
// operand; `*`, `+` and a literal divisor never widen, which is why the shape looks
// arithmetic-specific but is really about nullability. `859-nullable-retype-cures.loft`
// runs the three cures this message names, so the advice cannot drift from what
// compiles.

fn f(x: float) -> float {
  g = x / 2.0;
  g = x / g;          // float? into a float slot
  g
}

fn main() {
  println("{f(2.0)}");
}
